From: qforge
Subject: [PATCH] fix(CPU): keep the fastest cores online first

SetCoresEnabled walked the cores in number order and kept the first N
online. On big.LITTLE SoCs the little cores usually carry the low numbers
(SM6115: cpu0-3 are Cortex-A53, cpu4-7 Cortex-A73), so lowering the core
count in OpenGamepadUI took the big cores offline first: "4 cores" left a
game on the four A53s, at roughly 60% of the per-core throughput, when
the user only meant to save some power.

Order the cores by the scheduler's cpu_capacity, highest first, then by
number. On a CPU where the kernel reports no capacity (or every core is
the same) all cores tie and the old order is kept. cpu0, which cannot go
offline, is still counted first as before.

The kernel hides cpu_capacity while a CPU is offline, so each value is
cached the first time it is read: at startup, when every CPU is online,
and for the online ones before every change. Without the cache a second
change saw the offline little cores as "fastest" (measured on the Air X:
4 cores then 2 left cpu0+cpu1, two A53s).

---
--- a/src/performance/cpu/cpu_features.rs
+++ b/src/performance/cpu/cpu_features.rs
@@ -60,6 +60,13 @@
         }
         log::info!("Core Map: {:?}", core_map);
 
+        // Read every CPU's capacity while they are all still online (see cpu_capacity).
+        for cores in core_map.values() {
+            for core in cores {
+                cpu_capacity(core.number);
+            }
+        }
+
         Cpu {
             core_map,
             core_count,
@@ -215,9 +222,19 @@
             num
         };
 
-        // Collect all core IDs from the core map
+        // Collect all core IDs from the core map, fastest first. On big.LITTLE the
+        // little cores usually have the low numbers (SM6115: cpu0-3 are A53, cpu4-7
+        // A73), so walking them in number order kept the slow cores and took the
+        // fast ones offline: "4 cores" left a game on four A53s. Order by the
+        // scheduler's cpu_capacity, highest first, then by number; where the
+        // kernel reports no capacity every core ties and the order is unchanged.
         let mut core_ids = self.core_map.keys().cloned().collect::<Vec<u32>>();
-        core_ids.sort();
+        for cores in self.core_map.values() {
+            for core in cores {
+                cpu_capacity(core.number);
+            }
+        }
+        core_ids.sort_by_key(|id| (std::cmp::Reverse(cpu_capacity(*id)), *id));
 
         // Enable/disable cores based on their hyper-threaded sibling
         let mut enabled_count = 1;
@@ -266,6 +283,30 @@
     }
 }
 
+/// The scheduler's relative capacity of a CPU (sysfs cpu_capacity, 1024 for the fastest).
+///
+/// The kernel hides cpu_capacity while a CPU is offline, so every value read is cached:
+/// capacity never changes, and PowerStation sees all CPUs online at boot (Cpu::new reads
+/// them all) and refreshes the online ones before every change. A CPU never seen online
+/// counts as the fastest, which is the old number-order behaviour for it.
+fn cpu_capacity(number: u32) -> u32 {
+    use std::collections::HashMap;
+    use std::sync::{Mutex, OnceLock};
+    static CACHE: OnceLock<Mutex<HashMap<u32, u32>>> = OnceLock::new();
+    let cache = CACHE.get_or_init(|| Mutex::new(HashMap::new()));
+    let live = std::fs::read_to_string(format!("/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu{}/cpu_capacity", number))
+        .ok()
+        .and_then(|s| s.trim().parse::<u32>().ok());
+    let mut cache = cache.lock().unwrap();
+    match live {
+        Some(capacity) => {
+            cache.insert(number, capacity);
+            capacity
+        }
+        None => *cache.get(&number).unwrap_or(&1024),
+    }
+}
